1.0 Competition Description and Objective 
In this competition, teams will address a bioengineering challenge involving an older adult  requiring support with balance-related rehabilitation exercises. Teams are required to identify the  needs and challenges outlined in the Background and apply the bioengineering design process to  develop an affordable, feasible, and safe solution. 
The competition will consist of one (1) written case describing the rehabilitation scenario. The  case will be distributed at the beginning of the competition design time, and teams must develop  and submit their proposed solutions by the end of the competition design time. Physical  prototyping is optional. 
1.1 Team Composition and Eligibility 
Team Size: Must consist of exactly 2 competitors 
Eligibility: All competitors must be York University undergraduate students. Only Engineering  students can advance to the provincial level, OEC. 
2.0 Competition Details  
2.1 Resources  
Competitors will be granted access to the Bergeron Center for Engineering Excellence to develop  their solutions, and access to a virtual video conferencing platform to present to a panel of  Judges. Competitors are expected to arrive on time to present their solutions, from their home  setup. All teams are responsible for technical issues or mistakes on their slides or abstracts.  
2.1.1 External Resources Allowed 
Competitors can bring the following equipment with them:  
• Any background research conducted by team members prior to the competition  • Any electronic material stored on CDs, USB keys, disks or other storage devices.  Ensure to bring devices to access these.  
• Any textbooks, course notes or other reference material 
Note: Since the use of the Internet and other external resources is permitted in this  competition, all information used by competitors must be referenced carefully.  Competitors are not permitted to submit work completed by anyone other than the  members of their team. If there is any evidence that competitors are submitting plagiarized  work, the entire team will be eliminated from the competition and their home schools will 
be notified. Volunteers will monitor each team during the design process to deter teams  from cheating, however, competitors are expected to act in good faith with the spirit of the  competition.  
